    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Benelli Showed the World Its New Tornado Naked Tre 903 Concept at EICMA 2026 and It's a Triple!\nSubaru's New TY85 Manual-Equipped WRX STI Sport R Clocks Over 1,000 Orders in Less Than Two Days\n16 photos\nPhoto: Subaru\nSubaru doesn't need a limited-run special edition of the WRX to convince Japanese enthusiasts that a manual boxer-engined sedan with all-wheel drive is worth buying. Enter the STI Sport R, which attracted 600 orders on its first day on sale. By the following afternoon, it had passed 1,000 orders.\nSaid numbers come from Japanese motoring publication Creative Trend, which cites a Subaru dealership contact. Per the cited publication, there is a possibility that orders will be suspended on Sunday, November 22nd. The folks at Subaru originally intended a 1,000-unit initial production run, but Creative Trend understands that Subaru has increased it to 1,500 vehicles.\nIn any case, production figures will be revealed little by little in the following months. Back in October 2026, the company reported a year-to-date global production total of 647,145 units for the period through September 2026, representing an increase of 4.0 percent from the same period last year. Of the grand total, domestic production accounted for 363,770 vehicles.\nFor a Scooby that isn't a special edition, the WRX STI Sport R's opening salvo is quite impressive. The first-day orders alone amounted to almost twice the planned 330-unit monthly sales estimate for Japan, and this brings us to the WRX STI Sport+. Restricted to only 600 examples, the # was allocated through a lottery. At the time, Subaru recorded more than 9,000 applications for said 600 vehicles.\nThe attraction is fairly easy to understand. Japanese enthusiasts waited a long time for a regular-production WRX with a manual, and the STI Sport R's transmission is no regular manual. Referred to as TY85 , said heavy-duty manual differs from the TY75 offered in the US-market WRX in many respects, beginning with its higher torque capacity and mechanical rod linkage versus a cable setup.\nPhoto: Subaru Everyone who loves manuals prefers a more tactile and direct mechanical connection to the gears, right? Subaru's engineers also reworked the shift mechanism, cutting shift play by 60 percent for more precise throws. Developed from the Japanese automaker's Super Taikyu know-how, the auto rev-match feature matches engine speed as intended on downshifts for smoother transitions.\nOf course, you can turn off the system to heel-and-toe manually. Last but certainly not least, the TY85 is an ex-WRX STI transmission. Despite its origins, the STI Sport S uses a viscous coupler instead of the DCCD system we all know and love. Under the hood, it's not much of an STI either in regard to peak output.\nIts 2.4-liter turbo flat-four mill belts out 202 kilowatts, which should be read as 271 horsepower in the US or 275 in Europe, with Subaru claiming peak power at 5,600 revolutions per minute. 375 Newton-meters (277 pound-feet) can be enjoyed from 2,000 to 4,800 revolutions per minute.\nSaid numbers aren't nearly enough to frighten Merc's old-gen A 35 with the 2.0-liter turbo I4, let alone the S version of the A 45 and other compacts that received the 45-series treatment.\nBeyond its trick manual tranny, the STI Sport R also gets reworked electronic throttle and boost pressure control for improved response across the entire rev range. ZF electronically controlled dampers also need mentioning, plus Brembo six- and two-piston monoblock calipers working with drilled brake discs.\n5,480,000 yen is the starting price of the WRX, including the consumption tax. At current exchange rates, the Japan-only model costs just under 34,500 bucks.\nVia // Creative Trend\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n2027 Subaru WRX STI Sport R Subaru WRX STI Sport R 2027 Subaru WRX Subaru WRX Subaru Japan\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT What if BMW's New i3 Is Right and Everything We Know About Designing a Modern Sedan Is Wrong?",
